        "text": "We achieve the desiderata above by MAST, see the schematic in Fig. 2. Assume we have a sampled batch of n images, and an augmentation set T consisting of K atomic augmentation operators o1, . . . , oK such as RandomResizedCrop and Color Jitter. For each image x, we produce two augmented views v = t(x) and v\u2032 = t\u2032(x) by sampling random compositions of image augmentations t \u223c T(o1,...,oK) and t\u2032 \u223c T(o1,...,oK) with their specific augmentation parameters. We encode the views v and v\u2032 by an encoder f into their representations y = f(v) and y\u2032 = f(v\u2032), which are then mapped by a projector g onto embeddings z = g(y) and z\u2032 = g(y\u2032).\nTypically, a similarity loss is computed between z and z\u2032 to learn augmentation invariances. However, this makes it hard to learn distinct invariances because learning is unfactorized in the presence of the compositions of K augmentations applied on x. Even worse, unfactorized learning from contradicting augmentation compositions could cause feature suppression that hurts generalization.\nDisentangled learning is the main idea behind our MAST method to learn different invariances without feature suppression. Let Z = [z1, . . . , zn] and Z \u2032 = [z\u20321, . . . , z \u2032 n] be the two batches of d-dimensional embeddings of the augmented views V = [v1, . . . , vn] and V \u2032 = [v\u20321, . . . , v \u2032 n]. We build MAST on top of the VICReg framework (Bardes et al., 2022) which minimizes the embedding distance between different views, with two regularization terms:\n\u2113(V, V \u2032) = \u03bbD(V, V \u2032) + \u03b1\u2113var(V, V \u2032) + \u03b2\u2113cov(V, V \u2032), (1) where D(V, V \u2032) enforces augmentation invariance in form of the mean-squared euclidean distance\u2211\ni ||zi \u2212 z\u2032i||22/n, without any feature normalization. The variance term \u2113var(V, V \u2032) keeps the information content of each embedding (in Z or Z \u2032) above a certain 